"Orbits and the Magnetic Bottle | VirtualDemos",
"Orbits and the Magnetic Bottle",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Circular motion; Electron vs proton; E × B drift; Velocity selector; Mass spectrometer; Cyclotron; Magnetic bottle; Hall effect. This worksheet uses Circular motion and Magnetic bottle; the Hall effect has its own worksheet, The Hall Effect.",
"Drag the arrow tip on the drawing to aim and set the speed, and the ring to move the launch point. Speeds stop at \\(10^7\\) m/s (\\(v/c = 0.033\\)) so the nonrelativistic motion stays accurate.",
"Lay the ruler across the track: drag its two ends onto opposite sides of the circle. It reads to one screen pixel, so zoom in (wheel or +) for a finer reading. The timer counts time in the simulation and stops while paused. Start it as the particle passes a mark, such as the launch ring, and stop it after a whole number of turns. Keys on the drawing: M ruler, T timer, S picks what the arrow keys move.",
"The Magnetic bottle setup computes the field of its two coils exactly (Biot–Savart for each ring) and steps the proton through it with the Boris method, which keeps the speed exact.",
"Record reading stores what you read: the particle, the settings as displayed (q, m, v, B, E), your ruler reading and your timer reading divided by the turns timed. It never stores the model’s r or T. Cells can be edited; rows stay through setup changes until Clear.",
"Blind hides the r, T, f, turns, drift and pitch readouts, the r label, the circle’s center, the dashed path ahead and the period axis, and in the other setups the predicted speeds, landing points, \\(f_c\\) and \\(K_{\\max}\\). In the magnetic bottle it hides the largest field, the mirror ratio, the loss cone, the mirror prediction, the orbit radius and the period of one turn. The ruler, the timer, the settings and the speed and energy readouts stay.",
"Measure a charged particle’s orbit and period, then find the loss cone of a magnetic bottle.",
"A magnetic field exerts a force perpendicular to particle velocity (§{#}):",
"\\[\\mathbf F=q\\mathbf v\\times\\mathbf B.\\]",
"For nonrelativistic motion perpendicular to a uniform field,",
"\\[r=\\frac{mv}{\\lvert q\\rvert B},\\qquad T=\\frac{2\\pi m}{\\lvert q\\rvert B}.\\]",
"A magnetic bottle (§{#}) is a field that is weaker in the middle than at its ends. While the field changes little over one turn, the magnetic moment of the circling charge,",
"\\[\\mu=\\frac{mv_\\perp^2}{2B},\\]",
"stays constant. The speed is constant too, so \\(\\sin^2\\alpha/B\\) stays fixed, where \\(\\alpha\\) is the pitch angle between \\(\\mathbf v\\) and \\(\\mathbf B\\). A charge that starts at field \\(B_0\\) turns round where \\(B=B_0/\\sin^2\\alpha\\). If that is more than the largest field \\(B_{\\max}\\) on its path, it escapes: it is trapped only when",
"\\[\\sin^2\\alpha>\\frac{B_0}{B_{\\max}}.\\]",
"Predict the changes in \\(r\\) and \\(T\\) when \\(v\\) doubles.",
"Why does a magnetic field alone leave the particle’s speed unchanged?",
"A charge moves into a stronger field in a magnetic bottle. Its speed stays the same. Predict what happens to \\(v_\\perp\\) and to \\(v_\\parallel\\).",
"Follow the real apparatus limits for magnet current. Switch off before rewiring, keep magnetic materials away from the pole gap, and do not touch an energized electron beam tube.",
"<strong>Particle orbit.</strong> Choose the setup Circular motion (a proton, the Magnitude slider under Fields at \\(B\\) = {#} T, no electric field, perpendicular launch). Keep the particle and \\(B\\) fixed. Take four speeds: set the Speed in the page, v⊥ slider near {#}, {#}, {#} and {#} × {#}⁵ m/s (it moves in log steps, so use the value it shows). At each, read the diameter with the ruler, set Turns timed to {#} and time {#} complete turns, then press Record reading.",
"<strong>Magnetic bottle.</strong> Use protons at \\(v = 1.0\\times10^5\\) m/s, starting on the axis at the center, with the coils {#} cm apart and \\(B_0 = 0.20\\) T. Set the Pitch angle α at the start slider to {#}°, {#}°, {#}°, {#}° and {#}° and press Reset (beside Pause) to launch each. For each, record whether the proton escapes through a coil or stays trapped (several turning points), and for a trapped proton the bounce period. Then narrow the search in {#}° steps to find the smallest pitch angle that stays trapped. Repeat {#}° and {#}° at \\(B_0 = 0.05\\) T and watch the Magnetic moment readout. Record reading after each run; the bottle rows go to Table {#}.",
"<strong>Using the virtual apparatus.</strong> Choose the circular proton setup and enable <strong>Blind: hide predictions and answers</strong>. Turn on <strong>Show ruler</strong>, select <strong>Diameter</strong> under <strong>Ruler reads</strong>, and drag both ruler ends onto opposite sides of the track. Use <strong>Start timer</strong>, <strong>Stop timer</strong>, <strong>Reset timer</strong> and <strong>Turns timed</strong> to measure several orbits. <strong>Record reading</strong> stores your ruler/timer measurements; <strong>Export to worksheet</strong> sends them to the Data table. Plot <strong>r against v</strong>. For the bottle, choose <strong>Magnetic bottle</strong> and set <strong>Coil spacing</strong>, <strong>Field at the center B₀</strong>, <strong>Speed v</strong>, <strong>Pitch angle α at the start</strong> and <strong>Start off the axis by ρ</strong>; read <strong>Turning points so far</strong>, <strong>Bounce period, measured</strong> and <strong>Magnetic moment μ = mv⊥²/2B, now against the start</strong>. Raising the playback <strong>Speed</strong> to ×{#} or ×{#} saves waiting; it does not change the motion. In the bottle setup, <strong>Record reading</strong> stores the readouts shown and <strong>Export to worksheet</strong> sends the bottle rows to Table {#}.",
